{
  "rationale": "The binding constraint is accuracy, not throughput: 1 MSPS is far below what any family reaches (even iterative at N=22 and a pessimistic 100 MHz gives ~4 MSPS), so the throughput constraint is essentially free and the real problem is 'smallest area that still hits max_abs_err <= 2^-10'. Because accuracy depends only on (data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding) and is identical across families, the min-area design at every accuracy level is the family with the smallest datapath — the shared-datapath iterative core. So the Pareto front should be iterative-dominated, and that is where most of the budget goes. I hedge with the two next-smallest families (unrolled_k with small k, pipelined_m with large m) in case the cost model makes one of them competitive at some accuracy point, and I drop the fully pipelined family because pipelined_m with small m strictly dominates it in area. Ranges are centred on the accuracy threshold: output LSB is 2^-(W-2) and CORDIC angle error is ~2^-N, so both W and N must clear ~11-12; I extend W and N upward to trace the front out toward the HV reference area of 1500, and allow negative angle_guard / zero frac_guard so the optimiser can strip area where accuracy permits.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "Smallest-area family (one shared micro-rotation datapath with barrel shifters). Throughput is trivially satisfied at every N in range, and since accuracy is family-independent it should own the whole area/accuracy front. Widest ranges and the majority of the budget.",
