{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Both constraints are already comfortably satisfied everywhere they matter (0% throughput violations, best throughput 108 MSPS vs the 1 MSPS floor; best err 2^-23.75 vs the 2^-10 ceiling), so this is purely a front-coverage/HV problem, not a feasibility problem. The feasible front is densely populated but strongly one-sided: accuracy spans 10.8..23.8 bits (near its achievable ceiling given data_width caps at 28), while luts_plus_ffs only reaches 716 against an HV reference of 1500, and the whole registry was only ever sampled down to data_width=14 (front min is d=15). The covered area band (269..716 of 0..1500) is therefore a small part of the objective range, and the deep low-area corner that the selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) actually picks is thin - only the 269 and 316 points sit below ~350. A seeded NSGA-II pass over the full ranges of the families on the front (iterative, plus unrolled_k/pipelined_m where they contribute) is the right use of this round: it applies budget exactly where the staircase is under-sampled (low data_width/low n_iter to push the 269 corner down, and the 716..1500 area gap where higher-accuracy points can still add HV). Adding pipelined is not worth a round: it cannot win min luts_plus_ffs (its per-stage registers make it more area-expensive than pipelined_m, whose best feasible area is already 1465, and iterative is 269), its accuracy is bounded by the same (data_width,n_iter,angle_guard,frac_guard,rounding) parameters that iterative already maxes out at 23.75 bits, and its extra throughput beyond the 1 MSPS requirement earns no HV - so it would be dominated and the reserved mapping pass would only re-confirm that."
}

{
  "rationale": "Budget and round cap are both exhausted (400/400 evals, round 4 of 4), so the only admissible decisions are stop or infeasible. Infeasible is ruled out by evidence: 346 of 400 evaluations are feasible, and both constraints are met with wide margin — throughput best seen 108 MSPS vs the >=1 MSPS requirement (0% of evaluations violate it), and max_abs_err best seen 7.07e-08 (2^-23.75) vs the <=2^-10 requirement. The feasible front already spans luts_plus_ffs 263..716 against the HV reference 1500 and accuracy_bits 10.2..23.8 against the reference 10, i.e. it covers both objective ranges well past their reference points, and this round's hypervolume gain was only +0.8% (1.467e+04), indicating the front has converged. The spec's selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) is served by iterative [data_width=16 n_iter=15 angle_guard=-1 frac_guard=0 rounding=trunc] at luts_plus_ffs=263, accuracy_bits=10.2 (max_abs_err 8.29e-04 <= 9.77e-04) and 11 MSPS >= 1 MSPS, so a valid, constraint-satisfying minimum-area design exists. The unexplored `pipelined` family is not needed: its only advantage is throughput, which is already satisfied by a large margin by the cheapest iterative design, and it would cost area (pipelined_m's best feasible luts_plus_ffs was 1465, ~5.6x the front's minimum).",
  "next_plan": null,
  "decision": "stop"
}
